          "reasoning_content": "Key findings so far:\n\n1. Context (E0005): \n   - focus window log_loss 0.5435, alert true\n   - confirmation-1 log_loss 0.537996, alert false (below threshold)\n   - confirmation-2 log_loss 0.5129998, alert false (below threshold)\n   - deployment: reference_version == active_version (same hash), model_changed: false, preprocessing_changed: false, events: []\n\n2. evaluate_predictions (E0006): recomputed log_loss = 0.5435363482548169 matches alert exactly. So predictions are real, no data corruption in scoring — the alert value is genuine. 500 matched rows.\n\n3. compare_distributions (E0007): source vs reference_clients — all BH-corrected q-values > 0.05 (smallest 0.1303 for contract_type, tenure 0.4138). No significant marginal distribution drift. So no strong data drift signal.\n\nSo:\n- Model didn't change (model_changed: false, same version). → deployment_regression due to version change unlikely.\n- No data drift (marginal distributions stable). → data_drift unlikely as primary cause.\n- Log loss genuinely elevated in focus window vs confirmation windows (0.5435 vs 0.5380 and 0.5130). Reference value 0.5067.\n\nThe alert is borderline (0.5435 vs threshold 0.54123). Confirmation windows show log_loss back below threshold. This pattern — a single window slightly above threshold with no model change, no data drift — could be:\n- concept_drift (relationship between features and target changed) — need to check compare_conditional_relationship\n- random fluctuation / no_incident\n- data_corruption — but evaluate_predictions matched fine, no missing, distributions stable.\n\nLet me check concept drift with compare_conditional_relationship (reference_clients labeled vs source + feedback). This tests whether P(y|x) changed between reference and current. That's the key remaining test.\n\nAlso compare_model_versions: reference and active are same version, so they'd perform identically — not informative for cause but confirms no model diff. Given model_changed: false and same hash, I think compare_model_versions is not needed (would just return identical). But it could be useful to confirm. However budget is limited; concept drift test is more decision-relevant.\n\nLet me run compare_conditional_relationship(reference=reference_clients, current=source, feedback=feedback). Default columns all explanatory. folds=5.\n\nThis will tell if the target/feature relationship changed → concept drift.\n\nLet me do that.",
